// ===========================================================================
// engine/system/z80_init.emp — the Z80 idle program (no-sound builds)
// ===========================================================================
// The .emp port of the deleted AS z80_init.asm — the FIRST Z80 CODE module
// and the first live `module (cpu: z80)`. It is the Z80 program the boot loader
// copies into Z80 RAM when SOUND_DRIVER_ENABLED is undefined: it clears Z80 RAM
// ABOVE ITS OWN PROGRAM (`.code_end` .. $1FFF — the bytes it is executing out of
// are deliberately left alone), drains the register file through that cleared
// area, seeds a real stack pointer, and patches a `jp (hl)` at address 0 so the
// CPU idles forever.
//
// CANONICALLY EMPTY (the game_debug config-shape class): engine/system/boot_data.emp places
// the sound driver blob when SOUND_DRIVER_ENABLED is defined and this idle
// program only in the `else`. Both shipped shapes have sound ENABLED, so this
// module emits ZERO bytes canonically — the port is proven by an OFF-CANONICAL
// no-sound-shape oracle (z80_init_port), never a whole-ROM diff.
//
// The idle program is the corpus's only self-modifying code: `ld (hl), $E9`
// stores the `jp (hl)` opcode ($E9) at address 0, then `jp (hl)` jumps to it —
// the idle loop is that one-byte program the store just wrote. No SMC feature
// is needed; it is a plain data store of one opcode byte.
//
// The end-of-code marker `Z80_IdleProgram_CodeEnd` (three imm16 references: the
// ldir clear count, the store cursor, the stack top) is a trailing LOCAL label
// `.code_end` inside the proc — it has no external consumer (Z80_IDLE_SIZE =
// End - Start is a SEPARATE boot-cursor equate that these code bytes do not
// contain).
// ===========================================================================

module engine.z80_init (cpu: z80)

// The Z80's own RAM span ($A00000-$A02000 in the 68k view = $0000-$1FFF in the
// Z80's), hoisted to a name. Two operands below need it, and written inline the
// clear count spells `ld bc, (Z80_RAM_END-Z80_RAM) - .code_end - 1` — a LEADING
// PAREN in a Z80 operand is the classic indirect-vs-parenthesised-expression
// parse trap. Naming it removes the trap at the source rather than relying on
// the reader (or the next frontend) to disambiguate.
const Z80_RAM_SIZE = extern("Z80_RAM_END") - extern("Z80_RAM")      // $2000 (8 KB)

// OQ-D size mirror: boot_data.emp folds Z80_IDLE_SIZE numerically — its
// assemble-time cursor protocol cannot fold this cross-seam idle size off the
// link-external Z80_IdleProgram base (the row-52 wall). This module OWNS the idle
// body, so it self-asserts that the numeric mirror equals the number of bytes it
// actually fills; the whole-ROM assembled-anchor gate independently checks the
// body's span. A drift on either side fires here or there.
// 38 -> 40 on 2026-08-04: the SP seed at the tail became `ld sp, nn` (3 B) from
// `ld sp, hl` (1 B) — review item 27 finding 11. If you change the body's length
// again, this number and boot_data.emp's Z80_IDLE_SIZE move together or boot
// copies the wrong count.
// ⚠ THIS GUARD DOES NOT EVALUATE IN EITHER SHIPPED sonic4 SHAPE, and it is not
// broken. This module is `when = "sound_off"` (games/sonic4/map.toml), both
// shipped sonic4 shapes build with sound ON, so sigil reports
// `[module.unreachable] engine.z80_init ... its 1 ensure guard(s) are never
// evaluated for this target` under SIGIL_WARNINGS=full. Measured 2026-09-07:
// negating this condition leaves BOTH sonic4 shapes green AND byte-identical.
// It fires correctly under `--game demo`, which build.sh does not build on the
// per-commit path -- only tools/nightly_effects_gates.sh does. So the drift this
// guards against is caught NIGHTLY AT BEST, not at the commit that causes it.
// ⚠ THE MEASUREMENT THAT RULED THIS NIGHTLY WAS OF THE WRONG QUANTITY, and the
// ruling is WITHDRAWN. On 2026-09-07 this said: a demo build is ~150 s against
// a sonic4 build's ~160 s, so per-commit would DOUBLE every build. Those figures
// are real and they are `./build.sh demo` -- THE WHOLE VERIFICATION LANE. The
// question was what EVALUATING THIS GUARD costs, which needs only the assemble.
// build.sh's own header says so and I had read it: `sigil build (the ROM) 1.15 s`
// against a 38 s lane, "of which the assemble is 3%". Re-measured directly at
// aeon 6fb8fdc3: three runs of `sigil build --game demo` at 2.42 / 2.27 / 2.37 s
// under load 12-13 with two parcels building; the sigil lane measures ~0.75 s on
// a quiet machine. Either way it is ~1-2% of a build, not a doubling.
// Caught by the sigil lane, who re-derived rather than accepting the number.
// SO THIS GUARD SHOULD MOVE TO THE PER-COMMIT PATH, as a demo assemble or the
// `sigil build --check` mode landing on their side, NOT as a full ./build.sh demo.
// Re-opened as LS-16a; do not cite the withdrawn arithmetic.
// CLOSED 2026-09-08. It moved twice, and the second half is a REFUSAL:
//  (1) The demo ASSEMBLE landed at 7c64c6c1 (build.sh, "CROSS-GAME GUARD
//      EVALUATION"). This guard has been decided on the per-commit path since.
//  (2) THE `--check` SWAP THAT SENTENCE OFFERS IS MEASURED AND REJECTED. sigil
//      d90a297c is installed (af35fa56) and `--check` does decide this guard --
//      negated, `--check --game demo` exits 1 naming this line, while
//      `--check --game sonic4` stays green because the module is unreachable
//      there, which is the whole point of the row. But it is not a cheaper
//      SUBSTITUTE for the assemble, it is a WEAKER one. Measured on this
//      machine, load 2.3-2.7: demo assemble 0.745/0.757/0.756/0.763/0.767 s
//      against demo --check 0.404/0.411/0.421/0.406/0.406 s. The swap buys
//      ~0.35 s -- under 1% of a ~38 s lane -- and gives up everything --check
//      says it does not do. Demonstrated, not quoted from its banner: with
//      demo's `rom` region shrunk to 0x1000, `--check` exits 0 while the
//      assemble exits 1 with `section `math` [0xFDE,0x13D4) overflows region
//      `rom``. Nothing else on the per-commit path builds demo at all, so that
//      coverage has no second home. KEEP THE ASSEMBLE.
// The family this guard belongs to now has its own per-commit runner as well:
// tools/test_extern_guard_reachability.py (LS-16c) would have caught the
// silence that started this row -- it fails naming any file whose
// extern()-bearing guards no shipped shape evaluates.
ensure(extern("Z80_IDLE_SIZE") == 40, "boot_data.emp Z80_IDLE_SIZE mirror drifted from z80_init.emp's 40-byte idle body")

section z80_idle (cpu: z80, vma: $0) {
    // No register contract: Z80 contract checking is a rung-2 class (the 68k
    // clobber closure has no Z80 model, so `(cpu: z80)` modules are skipped),
    // and this proc in fact clobbers nearly every Z80 register. An empty
    // `clobbers()` would falsely claim it touches nothing; omitting the clause
    // declares no contract, honest until the rung-2 Z80 model checks it.
    @noreturn
    pub proc Z80_IdleProgram () {
        xor     a
        ld      bc, Z80_RAM_SIZE - .code_end - 1
        ld      de, 1 + .code_end       // (== code_end+1) leading-`.`local bug: see gap-ledger
        ld      hl, .code_end
        ld      sp, hl
        ld      (hl), a
        ldir
        pop     ix
        pop     iy
        ld      i, a
        ld      r, a
        pop     de
        pop     hl
        pop     af
        ex      af, af'
        exx
        pop     bc
        pop     de
        pop     hl
        pop     af
        // SP = $1FFE, the top of the Z80's own 8 KB RAM ($0000-$1FFF), matching
        // SndDrv_Init's SND_STACK_TOP convention (the sound driver's stack top —
        // never resident at the same time as this idle, since boot copies one OR
        // the other, so there is nothing to overlap). This USED to be `ld sp, hl`
        // with hl == 0, leaving SP = 0: the first push would wrap SP to $FFFE and
        // write $FFFE/$FFFF, which on the Mega Drive's Z80 map is the 68k bank
        // window ($8000-$FFFF), i.e. a stray write into 68k address space rather
        // than Z80 RAM. Inert today — this program disables interrupts and never
        // pushes — but it is the blob anyone copies to start a new Z80 program,
        // so it should not ship a booby-trapped stack. (2026-08-04, review item
        // 27 finding 11.)
        ld      sp, Z80_RAM_SIZE - 2
        di
        im      1
        ld      (hl), $E9               // patch: jp (hl) opcode at address 0 (hl == 0 from the pop above)
        jp      (hl)                    // idle loop
    .code_end:
    }
}
